\section{What this means in practice}{
Summaries that one study computed from the same delays are correlated, so
they are fitted jointly. Two are grouped when they agree on every column of
\code{\link[=as_epidist_estimates_data]{as_epidist_estimates_data()}} other than the summary itself, and a summary
supplied with its own \code{se} is fitted alone. A study that reported integer
date differences (\code{cens_adjusted} 0 or 3) is the exception: its mean and
standard deviation form one group and its quantiles another. One
observation is therefore a group rather than a single reported value, so
\code{log_lik()} and \code{\link[loo:loo]{loo::loo()}} report per group, and \code{loo} only compares
fits to the same studies and the same mix of individual and summary rows.
See \code{vignette("faq")}.
\code{\link[=epidist_meta_leave_one_out]{epidist_meta_leave_one_out()}} asks the study level question instead,
refitting with each study held out.

The sampling standard errors are plug in quantities that depend on the
parameters, so allow for genuine differences between studies with a term
such as \code{mu ~ 1 + (1 | study)} rather than relying on them alone. Supply a
reported \code{se} in \code{\link[=as_epidist_estimates_data]{as_epidist_estimates_data()}} for quantiles read off a
fitted distribution rather than the empirical data. Where a study reported
integer date differences, keep its mean and standard deviation and drop its
quantiles. \code{\link[=as_epidist_estimates_data]{as_epidist_estimates_data()}} warns for the studies the
approximations serve least well, and documents the two settings, \code{max_delay}
and \code{options(epidist.meta_n_quad = )}, that trade accuracy against speed.
}

\section{Advanced: an estimated growth rate}{
A summary row tilts its primary event, and weights the follow up of an
accrual design, by the \code{growth_rate} of its study. Where that rate is
\code{NA} in \code{\link[=as_epidist_estimates_data]{as_epidist_estimates_data()}}, or was given there with a
\code{growth_rate_sd}, the study estimates it as the \code{pgrowth} distributional
parameter instead. That is the parameter \code{primary = "expgrowth"}
estimates from individual level rows, so the two can share it. The model
adds \code{pgrowth ~ 0 + study} unless a \code{pgrowth} formula is given, and
\code{\link[=epidist_model_prior]{epidist_model_prior()}} sets the priors from the reported rates.
Summaries carry little information about the rate on their own, so share
the coefficient with rows that do inform it, for example \code{pgrowth ~ 1}
with a line list from the same outbreak. \code{vignette("model")} gives the
details.
}

